4","userType":"OTHER","userId":"2fe84a795c5348fa886d68a826161259"},{"organizationId":"8fafa3fd540c5564b98ef1e4e585ae68","sequence":15,"submissionId":20260001992,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Guillaume Legros"},"organization":{"name":"Sorbonne Université","location":"Paris, France"}},"id":"f53dc8b1919f41dc81ac03090ae5fac2","userType":"OTHER"},{"organizationId":"c08415067e2d5c528f0bb0252e358918","sequence":16,"submissionId":20260001992,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Augustin Guibaud"},"organization":{"name":"New York University","location":"New York, United States"}},"id":"91661d76cc284a9e87845248a0005878","userType":"OTHER"},{"organizationId":"2706897194df5e2a91ed29062ead9cc2","sequence":17,"submissionId":20260001992,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Nickolay Smirnov"},"organization":{"name":"Moscow State Technological University","location":"Moscow, Russia"}},"id":"08109382fc724b9e9a706711f81d5b66","userType":"OTHER"},{"organizationId":"bd23c8129c505cbe98cc1ada4faaed2b","sequence":18,"submissionId":20260001992,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Osamu Fujita"},"organization":{"name":"Hokkaido University","location":"Sapporo, Hokkaidô, Japan"}},"id":"17646508cb334fa890d2c1901932afdf","userType":"OTHER"},{"organizationId":"3853e3f89a4359c4b1613f7fc889324a","sequence":19,"submissionId":20260001992,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Ulises Rojas Alva"},"organization":{"name":"Slovenian National Building and Civil Engineering Institute","location":"Ljubljana, Slovenia"}},"id":"b54ab704a5134c8e8df6c889c41c7231","userType":"OTHER"},{"organizationId":"3853e3f89a4359c4b1613f7fc889324a","sequence":20,"submissionId":20260001992,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Grunde Jomaas"},"organization":{"name":"Slovenian National Building and Civil Engineering Institute","location":"Ljubljana, Slovenia"}},"id":"61b4e7a3ae4242f1bdf2628b7f05e3ce","userType":"OTHER"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Conference Paper","technicalReviewType":"SINGLE_EXPERT","modified":"2026-05-11T18:00:43.6761080+00:00","id":20260001992,"created":"2026-03-06T04:05:03.1941110+00:00","center":{"code":"GRC","name":"Glenn Research Center","id":"19eeb25fa404421d9eaa5f876e853e3c"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"The hazardous product release results from seven long-duration low-gravity fire tests are presented. These tests were conducted in low-earth orbit aboard a Cygnus spacecraft where the atmosphere pressure and oxygen concentration were varied to examine the effect of exploration atmosphere conditions. All of the samples were 40 cm wide. Four samples were PMMA and were 18 cm long. Two of these samples burned on both sides and the other two sample were attached to a plate that prevented fire on one side. The other three samples were cotton or a composite cotton/fiberglass blend that were 50 cm long. The cabin concentration of CO and CO2 were measured continuously in addition to the aerosol concentration. The aerosol concentration was determined using a light scattering device. The heat release from some of the tests was estimated using oxygen consumption calorimetry by measuring the oxygen consumption and the production of CO and CO2. These data were correlated with distributed temperature measurements in the spacecraft and the pressure rise. Overall, the pressure rise and temperature change were small but the smoke particulate rose to substantial levels and the gaseous products also achieved hazardous levels. ","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","cui":{"isCui":false,"submissionId":20260001992,"id":"176f394f24c34183a1044c4423d14081"},"meetings":[{"country":"PR","submissionId":20260001992,"endDate":"2026-07-16T04:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"meta":{"organization":{"name":"International Conference on Environmental Systems","location":""}},"meetingId":"d5cbfa4ab7eb4270b706f0ce345ea6c2","id":"0dc3ea5d63ce4887b8699c6e967f7b88"}],"name":"55th International Conference on Environmental Systems (ICES)","location":"Rio Grande","id":"d5cbfa4ab7eb4270b706f0ce345ea6c2","url":"www.ices.space","startDate":"2026-07-12T04:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"ICES Saffire 2026 05 04 b.pdf","notes":"Contract employers listed in corresponding footnote on first page.","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/20260001992/downloads/ICES%20Saffire%202026%2005%2004%20b.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/20260001992/downloads/ICES%20Saffire%202026%2005%2004%20b.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/20260001992/downloads/ICES%20Saffire%202026%2005%2004%20b.pdf.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":69.75972},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"GOV_PUBLIC_USE_PERMITTED","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":20170007283,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"5a629f42db984b09a6fe1b25d2e2775f","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Engineering (General)"],"keywords":["Aerosol","Spacecraft Fire Safety","Smoke"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":20170007283,"ear":"NO","id":"46cf89a157c544c5872e0f47b24d9520","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2019-07-12T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","otherReportNumbers":["GRC-E-DAA-TN32421","Report Number: GRC-E-DAA-TN32421"],"fundingNumbers":[{"number":"WBS 067463.01.99.99.99.99.22","submissionId":20170007283,"id":"3a4f193f187245029137f66d392b9c95","type":"WBS"}],"title":"Spacecraft Fire Safety Research at NASA Glenn Research Center","stiType":"CONFERENCE_PAPER","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2017-08-03T13:59:26.3630000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20170007283,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Meyer, Marit"},"organization":{"name":"NASA Glenn Research Center","location":"Cleveland, OH United States"}},"id":"349b3027b713410caff049182f866a53"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Conference Paper","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20170007283,"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2017-08-03T13:59:26.3630000+00:00","center":{"code":"GRC","name":"Glenn Research Center","id":"19eeb25fa404421d9eaa5f876e853e3c"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"Appropriate design of fire detection systems requires knowledge of both the expected fire signature and the background aerosol levels. Terrestrial fire detection systems have been developed based on extensive study of terrestrial fires. Unfortunately there is no corresponding data set for spacecraft fires and consequently the fire detectors in current spacecraft were developed based upon terrestrial designs. In low gravity, buoyant flow is negligible which causes particles to concentrate at the smoke source, increasing their residence time, and increasing the transport time to smoke detectors. Microgravity fires have significantly different structure than those in 1-g which can change the formation history of the smoke particles. Finally the materials used in spacecraft are different from typical terrestrial environments where smoke properties have been evaluated. It is critically important to detect a fire in its early phase before a flame is established, given the fixed volume of air on any spacecraft. Consequently, the primary target for spacecraft fire detection is pyrolysis products rather than soot. Experimental investigations have been performed at three different NASA facilities which characterize smoke aerosols from overheating common spacecraft materials. The earliest effort consists of aerosol measurements in low gravity, called the Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment (SAME), and subsequent ground-based testing of SAME smoke in 55-gallon drums with an aerosol reference instrument. Another set of experiments were performed at NASAs Johnson Space Center White Sands Test Facility (WSTF), with additional fuels and an alternate smoke production method. Measurements of these smoke products include mass and number concentration, and a thermal precipitator was designed for this investigation to capture particles for microscopic analysis. The final experiments presented are from NASAs Gases and Aerosols from Smoldering Polymers (GASP) Laboratory, with selected results focusing on realistic fuel preparations and heating profiles with regards to early detection of smoke. SAFFIRE is the upcoming large-scale fire experiment which will be executed in a Cygnus vehicle after it undocks from the ISS. ","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","meetings":[{"country":"Israel","submissionId":20170007283,"endDate":"2016-09-01T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"organizationId":"8bf72e8022f6474c8908c31bc079ddaa","meta":{"organization":{"name":"Technion - Israel Inst. of Tech.","location":"Haifa, Israel"}},"meetingId":"1527c46019234dc8a990e076af5002a5","id":"67aa466b18884d49b7df91baf56fe352"}],"name":"Space Studies Program (SSP 16)","location":"Haifa","id":"1527c46019234dc8a990e076af5002a5","startDate":"2016-07-12T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"publications":[{"submissionId":20170007283,"id":"a2009826a08c4e0ca9c141bc6f3e49cc","publicationDate":"2016-08-01T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"20170007283.pdf","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/20170007283/downloads/20170007283.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/20170007283/downloads/20170007283.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/20170007283/downloads/20170007283.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":59.486526},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribu