es"}},"id":"ea30f608fc904251aa39a0df3bf4a207","userType":"OTHER"},{"organizationId":"7354b6f97b02576fb9c66f60d484fa23","sequence":16,"submissionId":20210017780,"meta":{"author":{"name":"A. Carlos Fernandez-Pello"},"organization":{"name":"University of California, Berkeley","location":"Berkeley, California, United States"}},"id":"886a022537624b03bfe2133dcf01bb56","userType":"OTHER"},{"organizationId":"f977415493545e77b14135aeb0f0c0de","sequence":17,"submissionId":20210017780,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Guillaume Legros"},"organization":{"name":"University of Orléans","location":"Orléans, France"}},"id":"6066e9d35b294cb09ab9dd5d0b55d0f3","userType":"OTHER"},{"organizationId":"9f08003ab54c5afea3c8cbc306ff5100","sequence":18,"submissionId":20210017780,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Nickolay Smirnov"},"organization":{"name":"Moscow State University","location":"Moscow, Russia"}},"id":"5c6e47909c064a32bc8d80f50e85a75b","userType":"OTHER"},{"organizationId":"bd23c8129c505cbe98cc1ada4faaed2b","sequence":19,"submissionId":20210017780,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Osamu Fujita"},"organization":{"name":"Hokkaido University","location":"Sapporo, Hokkaidô, Japan"}},"id":"c3b0dd61c8c94ce2b564aff863125012","userType":"OTHER"},{"organizationId":"7ef25b3913855720818f6710ea6b8d94","sequence":20,"submissionId":20210017780,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Grunde Jomaas"},"organization":{"name":"University of Edinburgh","location":"Edinburgh, United Kingdom"}},"id":"c46aa7205be144f69f0fe98a3932ff75","userType":"OTHER"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Presentation","technicalReviewType":"NASA_PEER_COMMITTEE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20210017780,"created":"2021-06-21T18:39:25.6865110+00:00","center":{"code":"GRC","name":"Glenn Research Center","id":"19eeb25fa404421d9eaa5f876e853e3c"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"The spread and growth of flames over large solid fuel samples and their effect on the pressurized spacecraft were studied inside the Cygnus spacecraft while in orbit after departing the International Space Station. These experiments were developed by NASA’s Advanced Exploration Systems Division in the Human Exploration and Operations Mission Directorate. The ignited materials consisted of poly-methyl methacrylate (PMMA), cotton fabric and a cotton/fiberglass fabric blend. The samples were all 40 cm wide and with various lengths ranging from 18 cm for the PMMA samples to 50 cm for the fabrics. The overall results from these tests and their impact on the spacecraft are presented with emphasis on the fire safety implications of the results. The experiments included, a post-fire cleanup system, vehicle internal volume measurements, and transport of acid gases (HCl and HF). Measurements included video images, flame spread rate, flame temperatures and radiant heat output; energy release through oxygen calorimetry; distributed measurements of CO2 concentration and temperature at six locations in the spacecraft; CO2, CO, O2, HF and HCl concentrations; vehicle pressurized volume; and aerosol concentrations. Details of the flame growth and spread are discussed in other papers as are details of the post-fire cleanup system performance. The fire events had a measurable impact on the vehicle pressure, temperature, and carbon dioxide concentration. However, despite having heat release rates up to 10 kW, the average vehicle conditions did not rise to unacceptable levels. The combined results of the experiments provide significant new understanding of the impact of sample and flow duct height on flame spread and growth in addition to an improved perspective of the impact of a fire event on a spacecraft. ","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","cui":{"isCui":false,"submissionId":20210017780,"id":"dfedf41925a1435da4e653482ce3eda9"},"meetings":[{"country":"US","submissionId":20210017780,"endDate":"2021-07-14T04:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"meta":{"organization":{"name":"ICES"}},"meetingId":"34f38cc07d25408bb1a51d4f5504dabd","id":"0320323d3cbd44b0a7933db2186fc632"}],"name":"50th International Conference on Environmental Systems","location":"Virtual","id":"34f38cc07d25408bb1a51d4f5504dabd","url":"https://www.ices.space/","startDate":"2021-07-12T04:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"publications":[{"submissionId":20210017780,"publisher":"","id":"3b26f89fceb3489aafa9092b3bff894f","publicationName":""}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"Saffire ICES 2021 Saffire Final presentation compressed.pdf","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/20210017780/downloads/Saffire%20ICES%202021%20Saffire%20Final%20presentation%20compressed.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/20210017780/downloads/Saffire%20ICES%202021%20Saffire%20Final%20presentation%20compressed.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/20210017780/downloads/Saffire%20ICES%202021%20Saffire%20Final%20presentation%20compressed.pdf.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":44.338028},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"PUBLIC_USE_PERMITTED","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":20200000365,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"937a46f37dbe409b9bbb1698c0541449","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Space Transportation And Safety"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","eccnNumber":"","submissionId":20200000365,"ear":"NO","id":"9a2c9e05c09e44feb811019d125547ed","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2020-01-17T19:19:59.8670000+00:00","otherReportNumbers":["GRC-E-DAA-TN75331","Report Number: GRC-E-DAA-TN75331"],"fundingNumbers":[{"number":"089407.02.04.22","submissionId":20200000365,"id":"1d546fabe2b44663a09ede9ab4d9d844","type":"WBS"},{"number":"NNC13BA10B","submissionId":20200000365,"id":"6d4efd3587fe4e58a53c51d69944585b","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"}],"title":"Hazardous Product Detection and Environmental Clean-up Removal in Spacecraft Vehicles from Fire Induced Li-Ion Fires","stiType":"PRESENTATION","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2020-01-16T10:46:05.1700000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20200000365,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Padilla, Rosa E."},"organization":{"name":"Universities Space Research Association (USRA)","location":"Cleveland, OH, United States"}},"id":"7555c2c0d25643049bf5f2c82ef0e0d5"},{"sequence":1,"submissionId":20200000365,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Miller, Thomas B."},"organization":{"name":"NASA Glenn Research Center","location":"Cleveland, OH, United States"}},"id":"bfca5dbb4ab441b79db33227f499a9c8"},{"sequence":2,"submissionId":20200000365,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Dietrich, Daniel L."},"organization":{"name":"NASA Glenn Research Center","location":"Cleveland, OH, United States"}},"id":"1abf14244bfa4dfa9bea43a70016edd5"},{"sequence":3,"submissionId":20200000365,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Ruff, Gary"},"organization":{"name":"NASA Glenn Research Center","location":"Cleveland, OH, United States"}},"id":"9eb6456eead04f7db3246a2752ab7d68"},{"sequence":4,"submissionId":20200000365,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Urban, David L."},"organization":{"name":"NASA Glenn Research Center","location":"Cleveland, OH, United States"}},"id":"4cf790b5de8147188d26b65b46989b4f"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Presentation","technicalReviewType":"SINGLE_EXPERT","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20200000365,"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2020-01-16T10:46:05.1700000+00:00","center":{"code":"GRC","name":"Glenn Research Center","id":"19eeb25fa404421d9eaa5f876e853e3c"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"A fire on-board the International Space Station (ISS) resulting from a commercial Surface Pro tablet Lithium-Ion (Li-ion) battery can be detrimental to the spacercraft and the astronauts. The Spacecraft Fire Safety Demonstration (Saffire) program is focused on identifying and quantifying the risks that potentially arise inside a space vehicle by conducting large fire experiments inside the Cygnus cargo vehicle upon re-entry to Earth. The potential candidate that will be flown on the ISS is a 4- cell Li-ion battery pack Surface Pro tablet (42 Wh). The tablets were tested at the White Sands Test Facility (WSTF) using a localized heating method to emulate the failing mechanism that leads the unit into thermal runaway. Measurements inside the test chamber were performed on aerosol mass concentrations and for specific toxic products (i.e. from CO, HCN, HCl and CO2). These toxic products depend on the size of the fire and energy content of the tablet. Comparisons will be made with the Dell XPS (97 Wh). The concentrations are then used to extrapolate to laptop fires on a vehicle at the approximate volumetric size of the Orion spacecraft. The presentation provides an analysis on the detection capability and the response time to trigger fire alarms aboard a vehicle by using the mass concentration levels. These results will consider the rate at which the life support system is able to filter the atmosphere in order to provide a hazardous free environment.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","meetings":[{"country":"United States","submissionId":20200000365,"endDate":"2019-11-21T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"organizationId":"599569eabb93418db42fa10e3294bd52","meta":{"organization":{"name":"NASA Marshall Space Flight Center","location":"Huntsville, AL, United States"}},"meetingId":"82230c266c0840839e65f48e84ba8598","id":"53305a152e074b1c8622e42f9cbd165f"}],"name":"NASA Aerospace Battery Workshop","location":"Huntsville, AL","id":"82230c266c0840839e65f48e84ba8598","startDate":"2019-11-19T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"publications":[{"submissionId":20200000365